on a clear night the sky is full of  stars and constellations that humans  have gazed upon across the ages  but now some of these bright lights are  impostors  they're communication satellites moving  quickly through low earth orbit and  they're the first wave and massive  fleets that will deliver internet to  remote corners of the globe even as they  rewrite the familiar patterns in the  night sky  the leader so far is starlink a project  of the rocket company spacex which has  launched more than 1700 with tens of  thousands more on the way  other companies are hot on starlink's  trail and have begun building their own  large swarms  some versions of these plants call for  more than 100 000 of these satellites in  orbit by the end of the decade bright  dots creeping across our celestial  sphere  the companies promise to democratize  internet access for remote rural and  underserved communities but their  satellite constellations also pose grave  challenges to ground-based astronomy  in the early hours of night before these  low orbiting satellites disappeared  fully into earth's shadow  the initial starling satellites were  among the brightest points of light in  the sky  so bright that they threatened to  disrupt the gaze of sensitive optical  telescopes  as their light trails cross the  firmament also at risk are some of the  very communities that mega constellation  companies aim to help  indigenous groups with traditions built  around astronomy  with thousands of satellites in orbit  the night sky would fundamentally change  altering the stories that people have  written in the stars  starlink engineers have worked with  astronomers to find ways to dim their  satellites including by adding sun  visors to block reflections but it's  unclear if other companies will follow  suit and there are few regulations to  restrict commercial efforts in space  some legal scholars believe that space  is becoming a new arena for  environmental conflict and that  environmental protection laws could one  day be applied to low earth orbit  but those regulatory changes will be  years in the making  as space itself opens for business the  fates of precious science and storied  cultures hang in the balance  you
 

